# Build context for the BACKEND image is the REPOSITORY ROOT.
#
# It has to be: the `full` target does `COPY research/pipeline` to get the
# frozen feature pipeline, so a context rooted at backend/ could not reach it.
# The cost is that the context root also contains ~2.3 GB of research data,
# documentation and node_modules — none of which belongs in an image.
#
# This file is therefore written as an ALLOW-LIST: exclude each top-level area
# wholesale, then re-include only the paths a COPY instruction actually names.
# A deny-list would silently start shipping anything added later; this fails the
# other way, which is the right direction for an image that must not contain
# datasets or secrets.
#
# Exactly five things are needed by backend/Dockerfile:
#   backend/requirements.txt
#   backend/requirements-inference.txt
#   backend/app/
#   backend/scripts/
#   research/pipeline/
#
# The frontend image uses its own context (frontend/) and its own
# .dockerignore; nothing here applies to it.

# --- backend: only the app source and its two requirement files ------------
backend/**
!backend/app/**
!backend/scripts/**
!backend/requirements.txt
!backend/requirements-inference.txt

# Restated AFTER the re-includes, because last match wins. The generated
# product data layer (~130 MB) is MOUNTED at runtime, never baked in: it is
# rebuildable from the frozen artefacts and would make the image stale the
# moment it is rebuilt.
backend/data/**

# --- frontend: builds from its own context with its own .dockerignore. -----
frontend/**

# --- docs: reports and PDFs, ~84 MB, never needed at runtime ---------------
docs/**

# --- research: ~2 GB. Only the feature pipeline is copied; the datasets,
#     model binaries and predictions are mounted read-only at runtime. --------
research/**
!research/pipeline/**

# --- study-materials: role guides. Never a build input. --------------------
study-materials/**

# --- infra: pipeline definitions, ops scripts and docs. They CONFIGURE
#     the build; they are never inside the image it produces. -----------------
infra/**

# --- CI definitions ---------------------------------------------------------
.github/**

# NOTE — this list is enumerated per top-level area, which means a NEW top-level
# directory is not covered by anything and would be sent wholesale. That is not
# hypothetical: the team study-material folder was added after this file was
# first written and leaked 14 PDFs into the context until an entry was added.
# `infra/scripts/preflight.py` now FAILS on any top-level directory with no
# rule, so the next one is caught before a build rather than after. If you add a
# top-level folder, add it here too.

# --- repository-level files that are not build inputs ----------------------
.git/**
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.claude/**
tasks.py
Makefile
# Glob, not the bare filename: the inference overlay was added later and the
# literal `docker-compose.yml` entry did not cover it.
docker-compose*.yml
README.md
*.md

# --- never, under any circumstances, into an image -------------------------
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
**/*.key
**/*.pem

# --- build noise ------------------------------------------------------------
**/__pycache__/**
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/**
**/.ruff_cache/**
**/.mypy_cache/**
**/node_modules/**
**/.venv/**
**/dist/**
**/coverage/**
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store
